      "content": "I'm currently analyzing some particularly problematic data regarding the \"erratic\" nature of Finnish morphophonemic shifts. We are looking at how these small, discrete functional units—the morphemes—seem to interact with the stems in ways that feel almost... capricious. \n\nThe way these tiny components trigger sudden, sometimes unpredictable changes in the root's phonological shape makes describing them with purely dry notation quite difficult. I'm seeing a lot of \"interference\" where the presence of a suffix seems to fundamentally alter the identity of the stem in a way that is hard to capture formally.\n\nCould you provide a structural breakdown for these words, specifically looking at how these shifting, active elements seem to \"play\" with the root during the attachment process?\n\n1. kuka -> kenen (of whom)\n2. kuva -> kuvien (of the pictures)\n3. lampi -> lammen (of the pond)\n\nI'm interested in how you would describe the \"tension\" or the transformative \"agency\" of these small units as they force the root into a different grade.",
