      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I will provide a rigorous analysis of the phonological mechanisms involved.\n\nIn the case of lampi -> lammen, the genitive suffix -n is applied to the root lampi. The root lampi undergoes a consonant gradation process, where the palatalized consonant /l/ in the root becomes a palatalized velar consonant /ɬ/. This is a common pattern in Finnish morphology, where the palatalized consonant in the root is replaced by a palatalized velar consonant when the genitive suffix is attached.\n\nThe structural relationship between the stem and the suffix can be described as follows: the suffix -n is attached to the root lampi, resulting in the formation of the genitive stem lammen. The suffix -n is a genitive suffix that indicates possession or relationship, and it triggers the consonant gradation process in the root.\n\nIn terms of phonological notation, the root lampi can be represented as [lɑmpi], where /ɑ/ is the open front unrounded vowel and /mpi/ is the palatalized consonant cluster. When the genitive suffix -n is attached, the root becomes [lɑmɬn], where",
